\paragraph{Theorem D.2.16 (Proved: original odd-star fillings and all regular logs).}
Take one of the following marked configurations:
\[
\begin{array}{c|l|r|r}
 \mathrm{OS}&\text{ordered fibres}&p&b\\\hline
 46&I_3^*,I_1,I_1^\bullet,I_1&4&2\\
 50&I_1^*,I_3,I_1^\bullet,I_1&12&1.
\end{array}
\]
Set $P=pG$, $Q=G$, and place the degree-one linearization
zero at $I_1^\bullet$. Retain the original $I_j^*$ filling,
the primitive weight-zero nodal fillings and the hexagonal cusp.
At an additional regular fibre perform any free log of order
$m>0$ with vector $v=a e_1+b' e_2+c\delta+n\gamma$,
where $\gcd(m,a,b',c,n)=1$. Define
\[
 t=\gcd(m,n,a,b')\,\gcd(m,n,p,c),\qquad
 G=\mathbb Z/t\oplus\mathbb Z/m\oplus\mathbb Z/(m|n|/t)
 \quad(n\ne0).
\]
For $n\ne0$ the complete answer is
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \pi_1(X)&=H_1(X)=\mathbb Z/|n|, &H_5(X)&=0,\\
 H_2(X)&=\mathbb Z^b\oplus G,
 &H_3(X)&=\mathbb Z^{2b}\oplus G,\\
 H_4(X)&=\mathbb Z^b\oplus\mathbb Z/|n|.
\end{aligned}
\tag{D.T1}
\]
For $n=0$, put $T=\mathbb Z/t\oplus\mathbb Z/m$; then
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \pi_1(X)&=H_1(X)=H_5(X)=\mathbb Z,\\
 H_2(X)&=\mathbb Z^{b+2}\oplus T,
 &H_3(X)&=\mathbb Z^{2b+2}\oplus T,\\
 H_4(X)&=\mathbb Z^{b+2}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{D.T2}
\]
In both cases $H_0=H_6=\mathbb Z$ and $\chi(X)=2$.
All simply connected members, exactly those with $n=\pm1$,
have the oriented smooth type
\[
 X\cong \#^b(S^2\times S^4)\ \#\ \#^b(S^3\times S^3)
                   \ \#\ \widetilde\Sigma_0 N_m,
 \tag{D.T3}
\]
where the precisely framed twisted suspension and the spin
Smale manifold $N_m$ are as in D.2.14. In particular
$\widetilde\Sigma_0N_1=S^6$, so $m=1$ gives the corresponding
connected sums of sphere products.
\begin{proof}
\emph{The original filling, including its component action.}
The generator heights are $1/4$ and $1/12$ respectively.
In each case $P$ is narrow everywhere, $Q$ generates the
order-four component group at $I_j^*$, and in the second
case $Q$ also generates the component group of $I_3$.
Consequently the line $M=\mathcal O(P-O)$ has zero
multidegree at every fibre. As in D.2.12 the line
$M\otimes t_Q^*M^{-1}$ is vertical and has zero multidegree,
hence is pulled back from the base; its degree is the mixed
height $\langle P,Q\rangle=1$. The specified linearization
exists. The $I_3$ quotient is the actual primitive nodal
model, by its unit component action. The simple zero on
$I_1^\bullet$ gives the primitive hexagonal cusp.

Here is an integral topological model of the original star
filling. Let $E\to\Delta$ be the minimal elliptic-surface
neighbourhood with fibre $I_j^*$, $j=3$ or $1$.
It retracts onto the affine $D_{j+4}$ tree of $j+5$ spheres.
A section in the identity component has a holomorphic local
logarithm. Explicitly the local exponential sequence is
$0\to\mathcal G\to\omega^{-1}\to E^{\rm id}\to0$,
and its stalk $\mathcal G_0$ is zero at additive reduction;
see \cite[Section 2.4, (2.2)]{manUU21}.
Thus choose $R$ in the identity component with $4R=4Q$.
Then $Q_0=Q-R$ has exact order four and the same component
as $Q$. The path $Q_0+sR$, $0\le s\le1$, is defined using
that logarithm and induces an isotopy of fibre-preserving
translations on the entire minimal surface. These
translations extend over all components, as also recalled in
\cite[Sections 7.6--7.7]{manSS10}. The order-four invariant
section can alternatively be read directly from
\cite[Proposition 3.5(1),(3) and its proof]{manUU21}.

Likewise the narrow section $P$ can be contracted to zero
through its local logarithm. Topologically this also follows
from the zero component degrees of $M$: $H^2(E;\mathbb Z)$
is free on those degrees, so $M$ is trivial. A unit in its
contraction lift has a logarithm on $E$, since $H^1(E;\mathbb Z)=0$.
Straightening this unit and the two section logarithms gives
a smooth family of contracting quotients with marked boundary.
A uniformly sufficiently small contraction parameter makes
the whole family proper and free. Ehresmann's theorem with
boundary therefore identifies the original filling with
\[
 U\cong S^1_\delta\times T(t_{Q_0}:E\to E),
 \tag{D.T4}
\]
where $T$ denotes the mapping torus. Integral fibre markings
are transported through this family, so this is a statement
about the actual original filling and its boundary map.
The deformations are homomorphisms or translations of the
smooth torus groups; the origin section and fixed period loops
give the transported meridian and sweep marking.

Translation by $Q_0$ cycles the four multiplicity-one end
components. The incidence tree then forces it to reflect the
chain of $j+1$ double components. All component maps preserve
complex orientation. Its permutation on $H_2(E)$ therefore
has
\[
                  \rho=1+(j+1)/2=b+1
\]
orbits. Invariants and coinvariants of a permutation lattice
are free on its orbits. The mapping-torus sequence and the
product with $S^1$ give, integrally,
\[
 \pi_1(U)=\mathbb Z^2,\qquad
 (H_0,H_1,H_2,H_3,H_4)(U)
  =(\mathbb Z,\mathbb Z^2,\mathbb Z^{\rho+1},
                         \mathbb Z^{2\rho},\mathbb Z^\rho).
 \tag{D.T5}
\]

\emph{The full boundary kernel and the free defect.}
In the global $(e_1,e_2,\delta,\gamma)$ marking, the star
monodromies are, respectively,
\[
 A_*^{46}=\begin{pmatrix}
 -1&-3&0&1\\0&-1&0&1\\-4&-8&1&3\\0&0&0&1
 \end{pmatrix},\qquad
 A_*^{50}=\begin{pmatrix}
 -1&-1&0&-1\\0&-1&0&1\\-12&-24&1&3\\0&0&0&1
 \end{pmatrix}.
 \tag{D.T6}
\]
In particular $(A_*^2-I)$ has rank one: these are not
finite-order monodromies. Both $A_*-I$ and
$\bigwedge^2A_*-I$ have only unit nonzero Smith entries,
with ranks two and four. For $M_*=\partial U$ the Wang
sequences give $H_1(M_*)=\mathbb Z^3$ and
$H_2(M_*)=\mathbb Z^4$. The map on $H_1$ is onto the
$\mathbb Z^2$ in (D.T5), with primitive kernel generated
by the meridian, which bounds the extended origin disc.
Thus Poincare--Lefschetz duality and the relative sequence
imply
\[
 H_2(U)/\operatorname{im}H_2(M_*)=\mathbb Z^{\rho-1}
                                           =\mathbb Z^b.
 \tag{D.T7}
\]
The assertion is integral, since the map from
$H_2(U,M_*)=H^4(U)=\mathbb Z^\rho$ onto the meridian
kernel $\mathbb Z$ is surjective.

The two circle actions in (D.T4) give the entire normal-torus
kernel. One is $\delta$; the other is translation in the
mapping-torus coordinate with period four, using $Q_0^4=1$.
They sweep the extended origin disc, so their boundary tori
bound. Their orbit vectors on a regular fibre form the full
fixed lattice
\[
 \ker(A_*-I)=\mathbb Z\delta+\mathbb Z\ell,\qquad
 \ell=\begin{cases}(-1,2,0,4),&\mathrm{OS46},\\
                   (-3,2,0,4),&\mathrm{OS50}.
       \end{cases}
 \tag{D.T8}
\]
For an explicit check of the product marking, the integral
angular coordinate in (D.T4) is
$\delta^*-2e_1^*-e_2^*$ or
$\delta^*-6e_1^*-9e_2^*$ respectively. It is invariant
under (D.T6), is one on $\delta$, and is zero on $\ell$.
The minimal nonzero $\gamma$-coordinate of a fixed vector
is four, exactly the period of the second action.
Consequently the two normal tori project isomorphically to
the full fixed lattice in the Wang sequence. They form a
primitive rank-two subgroup of $H_2(M_*)$. By (D.T5)--(D.T7)
the whole kernel has rank two, so they are its entire kernel.
It follows at once that the remaining image equals the
regular-fibre specialization image; its cokernel is (D.T7).
This argument retains the star fibre's integral component
information. Replacing it by a product with an untwisted
elliptic surface, or by an order-two good-reduction filling,
would give a different boundary problem.

\emph{Global integral calculation for every affine vector.}
Insert $A_0=I$ before the four original monodromies. Set
$D=[A_0-I\ A_1-I\ A_2-I\ A_3-I]$, and use the direct
boundary maps $j_0,\ldots,j_3$ and
\[
 j_4(x)=-(A_1A_2A_3x,A_2A_3x,A_3x,x).
\]
The ten unchanged normal columns consist of two at the
star fibre, three at each of the two primitive nodal fibres,
and two at the cusp. Let their $16\times10$ matrix be $B$.
The exact constant integral certificates give
\[
 K=[B\ j_0(e_1)\ j_0(e_2)\ j_0(\gamma)],\qquad
 LK=I_{13},\qquad
 (Lj_0x)_{11,12,13}=(x_1,x_2,x_4).
 \tag{D.T9}
\]
The matrix $D$ has rank three with unit Smith entries;
$K$ is an integral basis of its kernel. The exterior
coinvariants are free of rank one, detected primitively by
$\omega=p e_1^*e_2^*-\delta^*\gamma^*$.
All ten normal tori are the marked fixed-loop sweeps proved
above and in D.2.12. The constant vertical form has zero
value on them. Thus (D.T9), together with a fibre class of
$\omega$-value one, specifies the integral splitting,
without an unspecified fibre-area correction.

The original degree-one vanishing lattices generate the
primitive $\ker\gamma^*$. All original meridians bound.
Van Kampen therefore leaves only $\gamma$, and the regular
log imposes exactly $n\gamma=0$ as in D.2.13.
After eliminating the ten unchanged normals, the entire
remaining torsion presentation is
\[
 C=\begin{pmatrix}
 -pb'&pa&n&-c&0\\
 m&0&0&0&a\\
 0&m&0&0&b'\\
 0&0&0&m&n
 \end{pmatrix}.
 \tag{D.T10}
\]
Indeed the smooth-log lattice is
$\Lambda'=\Lambda+\mathbb Z(v/m)$, with primitive relation
$mg=v$; its four normal tori have coordinates
$(-\omega(x,v),j_0(mx))$, and the fifth lattice generator
has coordinates $(0,j_0v)$. This is the same integral
calculation as D.2.13, with $p=4$ or $12$.
Its all-parameter local-ring Smith proof gives entries
$(1,t,m,m|n|/t)$ for $n\ne0$, and $(1,t,m,0)$ for $n=0$.
The $b$ additional generators from (D.T7) are free and
split off, since their quotient is free.

The degree-one Mayer--Vietoris source and target are free
of the same rank twenty. For $n\ne0$ their finite cokernel
has order $|n|$, so their kernel is zero. For $n=0$ the
cokernel and kernel each have free rank one; the latter
supplies the additional split free generator in $H_2$.
Thus $H_2$ is exactly as stated in (D.T1)--(D.T2).
The original star filling has Euler zero by (D.T4), and
the only nonzero Euler contribution is two from the cusp.
Integral duality, including its torsion terms, now gives
all the other displayed groups. In particular when $n=0$
one has $b_1=b_5=1$, $b_2=b_4=b+2$ and $b_3=2b+2$.

\emph{The exact smooth types.}
Assume $n=\pm1$. The free part of $H_2(X)$ is represented
in the original star filling. Its canonical line is trivial:
the canonical two-form on the minimal elliptic neighbourhood
is nonvanishing and translation-invariant, and its wedge
with $dw/w$ descends through the contraction. Thus $c_1$
evaluates trivially on every free generator. Since $X$ is
simply connected, $H^2(X;\mathbb Z)$ is torsion-free, so
$c_1(X)=0$ and $X$ is spin.

The global $\delta$ action is free on the original star
fill: in its contracting line model a nontrivial contraction
cannot equal a rotation. It is free on every other filling
except for the single cusp rational curve with normal
weights $(1,-1)$. This curve is in the cusp's fibre
specialization image, hence represents a multiple of the
regular class $s$. The normal relation $ns=0$ in (D.T10)
makes it zero. The equivariant nullsphere surgery of D.2.14
therefore gives a projectably spin free-circle manifold
$Y=X\#(S^3\times S^3)$.
Its simply connected spin orbit $B_5$ has
\[
 H_2(B_5)=\mathbb Z^{b+1}\oplus(\mathbb Z/m)^2,
\]
with primitive Euler class. Smale--Barden classifies the
oriented pair as
\[
 B_5=\#^{b+1}(S^2\times S^3)\#N_m,
 \qquad e=(x,0,\ldots,0).
\]
Apply \cite[Theorem A]{manGR25} successively, keeping the
primitive Euler class on the first $S^2\times S^3$ summand.
Its total space is $S^3\times S^3$. Each additional
$S^2\times S^3$ contributes its twisted suspension, which
is $(S^2\times S^4)\#(S^3\times S^3)$ by
\cite[Theorem B(2)]{manGR25}; $N_m$ contributes
$\widetilde\Sigma_0N_m$. Cancel the common $S^3\times S^3$
using the full Zhubr invariants exactly as in D.2.14.
This proves (D.T3), including even $m$ and its framing.

The program \texttt{manifolds\_star\_smooth.py} retains all
constant marked matrices and integer left inverses,
checks (D.T6)--(D.T10), and verifies 1,462 full $14\times15$
Smith forms, including 194 cases with $n=0$. The unrestricted
conclusion follows from the geometry and symbolic Smith
proof, not a bound on those parameters. No priority claim
is made.
\end{proof}

